From 51ffa978a2f445d318679497ab35491ea19964bd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Subbotin <ssubbotin@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 12:58:49 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/6] Add SIGFM (SIFT-based) matching algorithm for small-area
 sensors
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Add SIGFM, a SIFT-based fingerprint matching algorithm designed for
small-area sensors where NBIS minutiae detection is unreliable.

SIGFM uses OpenCV SIFT keypoint matching with geometric consistency
verification to compare fingerprint images directly, making it suitable
for sensors with capture areas as small as 112x88 pixels.

The algorithm is integrated as an optional dependency: when OpenCV is
available, SIGFM support is built; otherwise the build proceeds without
it. This follows the same pattern used for PIXMAN and UDEV optional
dependencies. OpenCV is resolved once, trying opencv4 and falling back
to opencv5, since distributions differ in which major version they ship
a .pc file for.

Image devices select the algorithm with FpImageDeviceClass.algorithm and
calibrate it with score_threshold, which replaces bz3_threshold now that
the score is no longer necessarily a bozorth3 one. Drivers that leave
algorithm unset continue to use NBIS.

A driver opts in with a 'sigfm' key in meson's drivers_info, which makes
it optional in the same way SPI drivers are when SPI is unavailable. No
in-tree driver sets that key yet.

Also add sigfm-bench, an out-of-tree benchmark that measures FAR/FRR by
driving sigfm_extract() and sigfm_match_score() over a fingerprint
dataset, rather than a reimplementation of the algorithm. It is not
built by default and not part of the test suite, as it needs a dataset
that cannot be shipped here.

Based on work by Natalie Klestrup Röijezon (MR !418) and Tooniis
(MR !530).

Relative to earlier circulated versions of this work, the matcher's
ordering defect is fixed (match::operator< was not a strict weak
ordering and silently collapsed correspondences), the surviving
correspondence set is capped at a fixed budget of the strongest
matches, FpImage and FpPrint now own independent SigfmImgInfo copies
instead of sharing one, both matchers validate the probe's print type
as well as the template's, and the serialized blob carries a magic and
version byte.

(cherry picked from commit d258eb51a00f7776a23476b8754fbc45176a9edb)
